---
// Privacy and rules: what is kept, who can see it, the cookies, and the rules.
// Drawn on the design canvas first ("Privacy and rules · phone", 9 November).
// Every sentence here says what the app does now; spec/pages.test.ts and
// spec/own-assets.test.ts check the ones a page can show.
import SlimHeader from "../components/SlimHeader.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SIGNIN_SECONDS } from "../lib/accounts.ts";

const signInDays = Math.round(SIGNIN_SECONDS / 86_400);
---

<Layout title="Privacy and rules · Hands Up">
  <SlimHeader />
  <main id="main" class="wrap info">
    <h1>Privacy and rules</h1>

    <section class="card panel" aria-labelledby="kept">
      <h2 id="kept">What is kept</h2>
      <p>Your account: a username, and your password scrambled so it can't be read back. Each sign-in is kept too, with when it started and ended.</p>
      <p>First-visit guides: your account remembers when you finish or skip the student and host guides. You can show them again from How it works.</p>
      <p>What you do: hands, Me too, questions and reports. A question asked since 9 November keeps a tag in place of your account, and your account sealed so that only the auditor can open it. Older questions have no sealed author.</p>
      <p>For a host: the session's name, its marks, when the recording started, and each Going over it.</p>
      <p>For the timeline: how many of those counted here said they were lost, minute by minute.</p>
      <p>The app logs requests, actions and refusals under a tag for your account or browser, never your username. A request's line has its path, status and time taken. Your IP address is used in memory to limit sign-in attempts, sign-ups and open pages, and is not logged.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card panel" aria-labelledby="who-sees">
      <h2 id="who-sees">Who sees what</h2>
      <p><strong>Anyone with a session's code</strong> can read its counts, its timeline and the questions the host hasn't hidden, without signing in. The pages also get how long each hand has been up.</p>
      <p><strong>The host</strong> also sees hidden questions and how many reported each one.</p>
      <p>The app shows no one else which account raised a hand, asked, pressed Me too or reported.</p>
      <p><strong>Whoever runs the server</strong> can link these to your account through the database and the logs.</p>
      <p><strong>The auditor</strong> keeps a key off the server, and with it can open who asked any question asked since 9 November. The tool for it writes the reason down first; it doesn't check that there was a report. It is meant only for reports of something dangerous. What it opens is an account, not a person.</p>
      <p>In a small room, a count can show whose hand is up. Anyone who sees your screen sees your username, your hand, your questions marked Yours, your Me too and your reports.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card panel" aria-labelledby="cookies">
      <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and other sites</h2>
      <p>One cookie tells browsers apart and lasts a year. Signing in adds one that lasts {signInDays} days or until you sign out. No ads, no analytics, and nothing on these pages is loaded from another site.</p>
      <p>Finishing or skipping a guide while signed out adds a cookie for a year. It remembers which guides you dismissed in this browser, with no account details.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card panel" aria-labelledby="where">
      <h2 id="where">Where it is</h2>
      <p>The app, its database and its logs are on Fly.io, in Sydney. Backups of the database hold the same records.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card panel" aria-labelledby="how-long">
      <h2 id="how-long">How long</h2>
      <p>Nothing is deleted on a schedule yet. Lowering a hand or hiding a question keeps its record. Taking back a Me too deletes the vote, and Undo deletes a report within a minute. There is no way yet to delete an account or a question.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card panel info-sun" aria-labelledby="rules">
      <h2 id="rules">Rules</h2>
      <p>Ask about the lecture. No threats, no harassment, no one's personal details.</p>
      <p>A report adds to the count the host sees, and the host can hide the question. A report doesn't reach the auditor by itself; a report of something dangerous can lead to the auditor being asked to open who asked it.</p>
      <a class="go" href="/s/HAND">Questions about this page? Ask in HAND, the open hall.</a>
      <p>Everyone can read HAND, so don't put your username there.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
